{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Ensemble methods typically use a fixed loss function to prune or weight learners, whereas DeepReason relies on argumentative warrants and coverage debt which are more semantically complex.","DeepReason's schools operate on discrete logical retirement, while ensemble methods use continuous weight optimization."],"overturn_conditions":["If a sandboxed Python simulation shows that boosting-style attention performs worse than uniform batch criticism at retiring rival positions.","If coverage debt is not analogous to loss in a way that benefits from boosting algorithms."],"shared_structure":["Both DeepReason's conjecture-criticism loop and ensemble methods in machine learning rely on generating a diverse set of hypotheses (rivals/base learners) and using a critical mechanism (criticism/voting or weighting) to combine or retire them.","In both systems, unbounded generation without effective pruning or weighting leads to inefficiency and failure to converge on a dominant theory."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_007","SRC_008"],"transfer_claims":["Transferring the boosting principle from ensemble methods: criticism attention should be focused on the most recently misclassified (high coverage debt) rival positions, dynamically adjusting the 'weights' of the criticism to target structural weaknesses."]},"claim":"DeepReason's schools fail to retire rival positions because uniform batch criticism distributes attention evenly across all 60 rivals, leading to coverage debt saturation. Optimizing attention by applying a boosting-style decay weight—focusing criticism on the highest-coverage-debt rivals—will increase the retirement rate.","counterconditions":["If a sandboxed Python simulation (sandboxed_python_v1) of 60 rival positions demonstrates that targeting a single critical counterexample per rival (counterexample-first) yields a higher retirement rate than continuous boosting-style coverage-debt weighting."